WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.420
- The headline is Roe vs. Wade
is dead, but is that true?

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.340
Well, that is mostly the case,

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.160
but the reality of this
particular situation

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.850
is so much worse than that
headline makes it seem.

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:13.950
And I'm not talking about that
from a political standpoint

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:17.120
of the idea that the
constitutional enshrinement of

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.300
a woman's right to choose
may be dead in America,

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.020
but rather the procedural machinations

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:23.240
of what happened in this
particular case are bad

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:24.073
for the rule of law.

00:00:24.073 --> 00:00:26.960
It creates uncertainty and
it hurts the legitimacy

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:27.940
of the Supreme Court,

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:30.490
regardless of where you fall
on the political spectrum.

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:33.580
The outcome in this case
is not good for anyone

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:34.670
because in the dead of night,

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:36.300
without any judge signing the decision.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.850
five conservative Justices on
the Supreme court overruled

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:39.690
the right to abortion.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.980
Allowing a Texas law banning
all abortions after six weeks

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:45.930
to go into effect with no
exceptions for rape or incest.

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:47.060
And to accomplish this,

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:48.970
the five Justice majority acquiesce to

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:51.030
a procedural trick dreamed up by

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.440
the Texas legislature
explicitly for the purpose

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.620
of evading federal judicial review.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.340
This isn't the way that
any cases are supposed

00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:58.173
to be decided.

00:00:58.173 --> 00:01:00.700
Let alone one of the most
important cases that has ever come

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:01.984
before the Supreme court.

00:01:01.984 --> 00:01:05.500
(dramatic music)

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:06.333
Hey, legal eagles.

00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:09.050
It's time to think like a
constitutional scholar because

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:12.170
in 1973, Roe vs Wade
challenged a Texas law,

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:15.060
that criminalized helping
a woman get an abortion.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.360
The Supreme court ruled that
the law was unconstitutional,

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:18.860
that women had a right
to an abortion during

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:20.070
their first trimester.

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:21.760
48 years later in 2021,

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.660
Texas passed a new bill
that outlawed abortion

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:24.970
after six weeks,

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:26.650
a clear violation of Roe

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:29.300
and the subsequent Casey
vs Planned Parenthood case.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:30.970
Rather than criminalizing abortion,

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:33.220
this time Texas gave private
citizens the right to sue

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.820
anyone who aids a person
in getting an abortion,

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.670
giving them a chance to win $10,000.

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:39.680
If they were willing
to narc on the abetter.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.250
The law also created a
procedural Rube Goldberg machine

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.000
meant to deny anyone
the right to challenge

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.210
that law in federal court.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.780
So what did the Supreme
court do on Wednesday night?

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:49.970
Did they overrule Roe
vs. Wade and it's progeny

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:51.660
without doing so explicitly?

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:55.050
And what happens to all
of the women who are stuck

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:56.890
in the middle in Texas?

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:58.560
Well, it's time to find out.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.270
So let's talk about a summary
of how the Supreme court got

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:04.910
to what is still technically
the law of the land today

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:07.070
and how the Supreme court
found a right of privacy,

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:09.340
which then led to a
woman's right to choose.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.310
Now, as we've covered on
this channel in the past,

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.030
the right to privacy is
not specifically mentioned

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:14.520
in the US constitution,

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:18.210
but according to still
technically current jurisprudence

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:19.820
from the Supreme Court,

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:21.980
they have found that that right to privacy

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:23.730
is implied by several amendments.

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:25.210
The first amendment
allows a person to choose

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:27.470
any religious belief and to
keep their choice private.

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:28.530
The fourth amendment protects

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.510
against unreasonable
violations of privacy,

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.170
government searches and seizures.

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:33.980
The third amendment
establishes his own a privacy

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:35.130
in a person's home.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:37.610
The fifth amend creates a right
against self-incrimination,

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:40.050
which therefore protects
someone's private information.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:41.980
The ninth amendment says
that the enumeration of

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:43.070
the constitution of a certain rights

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:44.250
shall not be construed to deny

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:47.130
or disparage other rights
retained by the people.

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:48.800
Courts have interpreted this as allowing

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.900
for broad protections of
privacy rights in ways

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:53.680
not specifically listed in
the first eight amendments.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:55.670
And the 14th amendment prohibits
states from making laws

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:57.470
that infringe upon a person's autonomy,

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:00.430
protections provided for
in the first 13 amendments.

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:01.710
Without this right to privacy,

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:03.870
states would be allowed to
prohibit married couples

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:05.040
from using contraception.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:06.310
It would criminalize certain kinds

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:09.010
of intimate sexual contact
between consenting adults.

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:11.040
It would ban teenagers
from using birth control

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.310
and it would outlaw
abortion because before

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:15.560
the Supreme court's decision
in Griswold versus Connecticut,

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:18.930
it was legal for states to
ban all of that behavior.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:21.220
Now, abortion rights in the
US are controlled generally

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:22.847
by two cases, Roe vs. Wade

00:03:22.847 --> 00:03:24.520
and Planned Parenthood vs. Casey.

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:26.180
This is a very, very brief summary,

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:28.480
but Roe struck down a Texas
law that criminalized aiding

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:30.140
a woman in getting an abortion.

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:31.640
The Supreme Court ruled
that this was a violation

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:33.540
of the due process clause,
the 14th amendment,

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:35.730
because it violated a woman's
fundamental right to privacy.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:38.500
First trimester abortion
became legal for any reason

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:39.510
and abortions in the second

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:41.130
and third trimesters were restricted

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:42.860
unless certain conditions, like risk

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:44.650
to the mother's life, were met.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:47.200
Now in 1992 in Planned
Parenthood vs. Casey,

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.730
the court more or less
reaffirmed the holding of Roe

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:52.670
that women had a right to
a safe and legal abortion,

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:54.440
but altered the standard for analyzing

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:56.000
the restrictions to that right.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.710
The court overturned the
trimester framework entirely

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:00.830
in favor of a viability analysis.

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:02.350
The court's decision also replaced

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:04.530
these strict scrutiny
standard of review required

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:06.420
by Roe with the undue burden standard,

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.280
under which abortion restrictions
were unconstitutional

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.900
when they were enacted, quote,
"For the purpose or effect

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.210
of placing a substantial
obstacle in the path of

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:17.410
a woman seeking an abortion
of a non-viable fetus."

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:19.680
Crucially before
viability, Casey says that

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.220
the state's interests are
not sufficient to interfere

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:24.040
with a woman's right to choose.

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:27.240
Which takes us to this
current Texas heartbeat bill.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.970
Here at issue is Texas Senate bill eight,

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:33.290
which effectively makes
abortion illegal after six weeks

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:36.620
with no exceptions for anything
including rape or incest.

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:38.740
And it doesn't even take
viability into account.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:41.980
This plainly contravenes the
holdings of Roe and Casey,

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:43.630
the legislature admits
that this is exactly

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:44.610
what they intended to do,

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:47.480
asserting that there is no
obligation to follow federal law.

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:49.390
Quote, "The legislature
finds that the state

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:52.710
of Texas never repealed either
expressly or by implication,

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:54.500
the state statutes
enacted before the ruling

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:57.300
in Roe vs. Wade that prohibit
and criminalize abortion

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.910
unless the mother's life is in danger."

00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:01.860
Under SB8, any Texas
resident can sue a person for

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:03.900
abetting an abortion and collect a minimum

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.850
of $10,000 plus attorney's fees.

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:09.850
Section 171.208 B list the
remedies permitted by this new

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:12.200
cause of action for these
private attorneys general,

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.010
if a claimant prevails in
an action brought under

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:16.670
the section, the court shall
award injunctive relief,

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:18.470
sufficient to prevent the
defendant from violating

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:20.770
this sub chapter or
engaging in acts that aid

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:22.570
or abet violations of the sub chapter.

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:25.420
Two, statutory damages in an
amount not less than $10,000

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:27.540
for each abortion that
the defendant performed

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:29.750
or induced in violation of the sub chapter

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:31.740
and for each abortion performed or induced

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:33.230
in violation of the sub chapter,

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:34.800
the defendant aided and abetted.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:36.970
And three, costs and attorney's fees.

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:38.450
And the last, one costs
and attorney's fees,

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:41.320
is one way, only successful
plaintiffs are entitled

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:42.930
to costs and attorney's fees.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:44.430
Successful defendants are not,

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:46.420
it is a one-way fee shifting provision.

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:48.340
Now this law was written
very strategically.

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:51.040
It doesn't allow private bounty
hunters to sue the women,

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:53.270
but it's written very broadly
so that anyone who helps

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:55.110
a pregnant person could be sued.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:58.070
Anyone who pays for or
reimburses cost of an abortion

00:05:58.070 --> 00:06:00.500
through insurance or otherwise
can be in an abetter.

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:03.220
This means a person's friends,
family, doctor, counselor,

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:04.920
minister, could be sued.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:06.610
This could be an Uber driver or a person

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:08.540
who contributes funds for an abortion.

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:09.380
This is also of course,

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:11.260
an attack on employees
of abortion clinics.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:13.170
Since anyone who works there is liable

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:17.590
if they defy this law and help
induce an abortion to occur.

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:20.530
In the event, a person successfully
sues abortion provider,

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:22.950
the court must permanently shut it down.

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:24.660
And if the defendant in one of these cases

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:26.070
actually prevails, they're not permitted

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:27.800
to seek attorney's fees
from the other side,

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:28.890
only the plaintiffs are.

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:31.810
As Justice Sotomayer
explained in her dissent,

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:33.760
the law quote, deputizes
the state's citizens

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:36.190
as bounty hunters,
offering them cash prizes

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:39.440
for civilly prosecuting their
neighbor's medical procedures.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:41.690
So there are obviously
plenty of things about SB8

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:44.670
that we would ordinarily expect
to be struck down in court

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:46.690
after a full hearing on
the merits of the law.

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:49.950
It's clearly in violation of
Planned Parenthood vs. Casey.

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:52.030
But that's not what the
Supreme court did here.

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:55.060
Instead, they took advantage
of a Kafka-esque procedure

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:57.100
created by the Texas legislature.

00:06:57.100 --> 00:06:59.410
The Supreme Court's 1908
decision in ex parte young,

00:06:59.410 --> 00:07:00.890
established that someone raising

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:03.720
a constitutional challenge
to a state law can sue

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.230
the state officer charged
with enforcing that law.

00:07:06.230 --> 00:07:08.800
So usually when a state
passes an anti-abortion law,

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:11.680
abortion providers file a
pre enforcement suit against

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:13.110
the state officials in federal court,

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:14.610
challenging its constitutionality.

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:16.220
They are allowed to file this suit because

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:18.490
if an unconstitutional
law goes into effect,

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:20.920
it will immediately
curtail individual rights.

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:23.230
And when a district court
grants a preliminary injunction,

00:07:23.230 --> 00:07:24.900
the law never actually goes into effect.

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:27.210
This has happened
repeatedly over the decades,

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:29.630
frustrating conservative
attempts to overturn Roe.

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:31.210
So how did the Texas law get

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:33.830
around federal court deference to Roe?

00:07:33.830 --> 00:07:36.070
Well, it eliminated the
state officials from

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:37.410
the equation so that there aren't

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:39.130
any state officials who can be sued.

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:39.997
The law states, quote,

00:07:39.997 --> 00:07:42.410
"The requirement of the
subchapter shall be enforced

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:45.230
exclusively through
private civil actions."

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:48.330
The law can be enforced by
anyone who is against abortion,

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:51.130
but it can't be enforced by
state or local officials.

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:53.400
Meanwhile, abortion clinics,
can't sue the state of Texas

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:57.010
because no state official has
the power to enforce this law.

00:07:57.010 --> 00:07:58.950
And if a court entered
an injunction against

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:00.660
the enforcement of the law,

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:02.310
the argument is that it would be null

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:03.880
because state officials have nothing

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:05.890
to do with enforcing this law.

00:08:05.890 --> 00:08:07.840
The Texas attorney general, and
other state officials, can't

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:11.000
enforce this law, so
theoretically they can't be sued.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:12.890
Other common targets can't be sued either.

00:08:12.890 --> 00:08:14.940
The governor has no
power to enforce the law.

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:16.170
So he can't be sued.

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:17.540
You can't sue the Texas legislature

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:19.100
because they have legislative immunity.

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:21.910
So the only litigants who
can challenge SB8 are people

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:24.690
and organizations who are
actually sued in civil court.

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:27.410
But it's not clear whether a
person who is sued can actually

00:08:27.410 --> 00:08:29.530
say that the law is unconstitutional.

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:32.810
That's because section 171.208 E,

00:08:32.810 --> 00:08:34.500
eliminates several defenses,

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:36.110
one, ignorance or mistake of the law,

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:37.670
two, defendant's belief
that the requirements

00:08:37.670 --> 00:08:39.130
of the sub chapter are unconstitutional

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:40.660
or were unconstitutional,

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:43.040
three, a defense reliance
on any court decision

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:46.300
that has been overruled on
appeal or by subsequent court.

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:48.380
Even if that court decision
had not been overruled

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:50.040
when the defendant engaged in conduct

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:51.740
that violates the sub chapter,

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:53.790
four, defendant's reliance on any state

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:55.490
or federal court decision
that's not binding on

00:08:55.490 --> 00:08:57.140
this court in which the
action has been brought,

00:08:57.140 --> 00:08:59.000
five, non-mutual issue preclusion

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:01.230
or non-mutual claim preclusion,

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:03.370
six, the consent of the
unborn child's mother to

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:04.450
the abortion and seven,

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:06.370
any claim that the
enforcement of the sub chapter

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:07.960
or the imposition of
civil liability against

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:09.740
the defendant will violate
the constitutional rights

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:13.490
of third parties, except
as provided by 171.209

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:17.950
and section 171.209 expressly
denies third party standing

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:18.920
in state court.

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:21.080
Texas has argued that since a
pregnant women can't be sued,

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:23.780
a pregnant person lacks
standing to challenge the law.

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:25.300
And under this law, pregnant women

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:26.960
are deprived of any agency.

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:28.500
And a third party like
an insurance company

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:30.410
or abortion rights
group also cannot invoke

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:31.720
the constitutional rights of a third party

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:33.800
unless the Supreme Court
gives them standing.

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:37.230
And note that this law is
specifically taking aim

00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:41.260
at the current exstance
jurisprudence on the Supreme court

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:44.650
and says that if Roe or
Casey is overturned by

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:47.612
the Supreme court, you
weren't allowed to rely on

00:09:47.612 --> 00:09:50.082
the Supreme court jurisprudence

00:09:50.082 --> 00:09:51.840
because it was later overturned.

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:54.220
So there's absolutely no doubt
whatsoever that this law was

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:55.920
written specifically with an intent

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:58.240
towards avoiding federal oversight.

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:01.500
It's sort of evil genius in that respect.

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:04.430
And normally that wouldn't
necessarily be an issue,

00:10:04.430 --> 00:10:07.190
but the Supreme court, as we'll
talk about in just a minute,

00:10:07.190 --> 00:10:11.500
has acquiesced to the gamesmanship
of this particular bill

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:14.220
in a way that I don't think
I've ever seen before,

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:16.530
but let's talk about the
procedural history of what happened

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:18.310
when this law was passed,

00:10:18.310 --> 00:10:20.620
because that raises questions too.

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:22.190
Texas Governor Greg Abbott signed

00:10:22.190 --> 00:10:24.300
the Texas law on May 19th, 2021.

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:26.070
The law is known as a heartbeat bill,

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:28.190
which bans doctors from
performing abortions

00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:30.270
once they can detect a
fetal heartbeat around

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:31.970
the sixth week of pregnancy.

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:33.910
Over 15 states have
enacted heartbeat bills

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:35.690
and federal courts have
struck down each one

00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:37.750
because they violate the right
to an abortion established

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:38.660
by Roe and Casey.

00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:40.260
And despite their unconstitutionality,

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:42.270
legislatures keep passing the bills.

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:45.070
Texas added these unique
provisions to the new law,

00:10:45.070 --> 00:10:47.780
which allow private citizens
to collect civil bounties

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:50.410
for snitching on anyone
that abets an abortion.

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:52.528
As it aside, there are
many laws like these on

00:10:52.528 --> 00:10:55.020
the books, they're called
private attorney general laws,

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:58.150
where someone who isn't
personally affected by

00:10:58.150 --> 00:11:01.270
the law can sue on someone else's behalf.

00:11:01.270 --> 00:11:03.720
If there is indeed a violation of the law.

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:06.010
Generally it has been
democratic legislatures

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:08.840
that have passed private
attorneys generals law,

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:12.370
because they allow individuals to enforce

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:15.130
the law against generally
large corporations.

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:17.260
And you often see this
in a class context where

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:19.330
there are small violations
that you aggregate

00:11:19.330 --> 00:11:21.150
on behalf of a class of people.

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:26.150
And often it was generally
the conservative executives

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:28.730
that railed against these particular laws.

00:11:28.730 --> 00:11:30.930
These were the kinds of things
that president Reagan railed

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:33.490
against saying that really the
only people that made money

00:11:33.490 --> 00:11:36.060
were the trial lawyers
and that we needed things

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:37.020
like tort reform.

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:39.150
So if nothing else that
is perhaps ironic that

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:42.210
the Texas legislature has
adopted this particular tool.

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:43.680
Now in July 2021,

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:46.120
Texas abortion providers
sued to enjoin the law before

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:47.800
it became effective on September 1st.

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:49.580
Since the providers
can't sue state officials

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:51.240
and the legislature
has immunity from suit,

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:53.650
the provider sued the court
personnel who are responsible

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:55.150
for enforcing the law.

00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:57.520
A state judge, and a county court clerk.

00:11:57.520 --> 00:12:00.250
They also sued Mark Dixon,
an anti-abortion activists

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:02.680
who, plaintiff's assume, would
be one of the first people

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:04.750
to file lawsuits to collect the bounties.

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:07.380
Now that's generally a no-no
because courts are not in

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:10.240
the business of offering
perspective opinions.

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:12.420
And the court clerk,
judge and activist filed

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:14.060
a motion to dismiss,

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:15.880
but the federal district
court Judge Pittman,

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:18.800
presided and denied the
motion on August 25th,

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:20.490
allowing the suit to go forward.

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:22.010
The federal court agreed
with plaintiffs that

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:23.010
the defense would in fact,

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:24.400
play a role in enforcing the statutes,

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:27.080
either by filing a lawsuit
or by adjudicating them,

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:29.170
the defendants quickly filed
an appeal on the motion

00:12:29.170 --> 00:12:30.590
to dismiss with the fifth circuit,

00:12:30.590 --> 00:12:33.320
the court of appeals
that hears the appeals

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:34.153
from the district court.

00:12:34.153 --> 00:12:36.280
The defendants also
wanted the appeals court

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:37.970
to halt other proceedings
in the district court,

00:12:37.970 --> 00:12:40.100
including a hearing on
the challengers request

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:42.140
for preliminary injunction
that was scheduled

00:12:42.140 --> 00:12:43.110
for August 30th.

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:44.310
Now, up until this point,

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:46.590
this was a fairly standard
trip to the court of appeals

00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:48.530
in a pre enforcement action.

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:51.380
However, this is where
the normal process ended.

00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:53.740
The fifth circuit granted
the defendant's requests,

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:56.430
it canceled the upcoming
hearing and granted the motion

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:58.110
to stop the district court proceedings

00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:00.120
and then denied the plaintiff's request

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:01.440
to expedite the appeal.

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:02.273
So in other words,

00:13:02.273 --> 00:13:04.440
the fifth circuit stopped
the district court

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:06.780
from stopping the law
from going into effect.

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:08.660
And the fifth circuit declined to explain

00:13:08.660 --> 00:13:10.380
the basis for its ruling.

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:12.450
So the plaintiffs had
to appeal directly to

00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:14.400
the Supreme Court for
an injunction in joining

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:16.020
the law from going into effect while

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:17.460
the legal case wound its way through

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:19.120
the courts for a marriage decision.

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:22.030
Remember this point, no marriage
decision has been decided.

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:24.510
A full decision SB8
would normally take years

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:26.830
to resolve by the district court,

00:13:26.830 --> 00:13:28.520
the court of appeals of the fifth circuit

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:29.920
and then the Supreme Court.

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:32.510
So with this request in
front of the Supreme Court,

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:34.720
under this procedure, the
emergency motion first went

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:37.720
to Justice Alito who was assigned
to hear emergency requests

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:38.553
from Texas.

00:13:38.553 --> 00:13:40.080
Well, Alito had the
right to act on his own

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:41.300
or refer to the full court.

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:43.800
And he chose to refer it
to the full Supreme Court.

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:46.570
There, one day after the
law went into effect,

00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:49.050
a five Justice majority
ruled that there was no harm

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:50.790
that justified in emergency stay.

00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:53.740
The unsigned order from
the Supreme Court stated

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:55.590
that it was uncertain
whether the court clerk

00:13:55.590 --> 00:13:57.920
and judge would be asked to
enforce the new abortion law.

00:13:57.920 --> 00:13:59.990
Basically they were saying
that they had no idea

00:13:59.990 --> 00:14:02.470
whether anti-abortion activists
who had waited over 40 years

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:04.500
for this moment would not necessarily seek

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:06.310
to enforce the law immediately.

00:14:06.310 --> 00:14:08.340
This, by the way, is part of the courts,

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:10.960
probably unfairly named shadow docket.

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:11.793
In other words,

00:14:11.793 --> 00:14:13.920
this isn't a full appeal
to the Supreme court.

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:16.780
It's an emergency application
that often these things

00:14:16.780 --> 00:14:19.010
are decided just by not taking them up.

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:22.330
It's actually fairly rare
to get a decision from

00:14:22.330 --> 00:14:24.750
the majority when they
say that they're not going

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:26.810
to grant a injunctive relief.

00:14:26.810 --> 00:14:28.860
And what they do in this
particular case is they basically

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:32.260
say that while the law is
probably unconstitutional,

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:34.850
there are some procedural
quirks that are not going

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:37.120
to allow them to get to
these questions right now.

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:38.810
And they just don't see the need

00:14:38.810 --> 00:14:41.610
to grant injunctive emergency relief.

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:44.550
And here is the entirety of
the Supreme court's decision.

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:49.050
It's about 500 words from this
unsigned five conservative

00:14:49.050 --> 00:14:49.960
Justice majority.

00:14:49.960 --> 00:14:51.960
Quote, "The application
for injunctive relief or in

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:53.500
the alternative, to vacate the stays of

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:55.940
the district court proceedings
presented to Justice Alito,

00:14:55.940 --> 00:14:58.110
and by him referred to
the court is denied.

00:14:58.110 --> 00:15:01.500
To prevail in an application
for a stay or an injunction,

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:03.130
an applicant must carry
the burden of making

00:15:03.130 --> 00:15:05.820
a strong showing that it is
likely to succeed on the merits

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:07.440
and that it will be irreparably injured,

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:10.170
absent a stay, that the balance
of the equities favors it

00:15:10.170 --> 00:15:12.500
and that a stay is consistent
with a public interest."

00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:13.420
So far, so good.

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:14.360
That's completely normal.

00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:17.540
That is the standard for a
temporary restraining order in

00:15:17.540 --> 00:15:18.917
an emergency context like this.

00:15:18.917 --> 00:15:21.390
"The applicants now before is
have raised serious questions

00:15:21.390 --> 00:15:23.720
regarding constitutionality
on the Texas law at issue."

00:15:23.720 --> 00:15:24.567
Yeah, no kidding.

00:15:24.567 --> 00:15:26.760
"But their application
also presents complex

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:28.560
and novel antecedent procedural questions

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:30.480
on which they have not
carried their burden.

00:15:30.480 --> 00:15:32.420
For example, federal
courts enjoy the power

00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:35.530
to enjoin individuals
tasked with enforcing laws,

00:15:35.530 --> 00:15:36.860
not the laws themselves.

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:38.670
And it is unclear whether
the named defendants

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:40.690
in this lawsuit can or
will seek to enforce

00:15:40.690 --> 00:15:42.860
the Texas law against the
applicants in a manner

00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:44.550
that might permit our intervention.

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:46.440
The state has represented
that neither it nor

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:48.114
its executive employees
possess the authority

00:15:48.114 --> 00:15:51.050
to enforce the Texas law
either directly or indirectly,

00:15:51.050 --> 00:15:52.870
nor is it clear whether
under existing precedent,

00:15:52.870 --> 00:15:55.430
the court can issue an
injunction against state judges

00:15:55.430 --> 00:15:57.920
asked to decide a lawsuit under Texas law.

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:00.070
Finally, the sole private
citizen respondent

00:16:00.070 --> 00:16:01.640
before us has filed an affidavit stating

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:04.130
that he has no present
intention to enforce the law.

00:16:04.130 --> 00:16:05.830
In light of such issues, we cannot say

00:16:05.830 --> 00:16:07.630
that applicants have met
their burden to prevail

00:16:07.630 --> 00:16:09.780
in an injunction or stay application.

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:10.930
In reaching this conclusion,

00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:13.140
we stress that we do not
purport to resolve definitively

00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:14.900
any jurisdictional or substantive claim in

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:16.210
the applicant's lawsuit.

00:16:16.210 --> 00:16:17.840
In particular, this order is not based on

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:20.220
any conclusion about the
constitutionality of Texas's law

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:22.920
and in no way limits other
procedurally proper challenges

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:26.050
to Texas's law, including
in Texas state courts."

00:16:26.050 --> 00:16:27.870
This decision raises so many questions.

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:30.130
First can plaintiffs
actually sue the court clerk

00:16:30.130 --> 00:16:31.860
and judge in this kind of situation?

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:33.500
Justice Briar's descent suggested

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:34.760
the answer would certainly be yes,

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:37.410
since you can't have a right
without a remedy to enforce it.

00:16:37.410 --> 00:16:38.710
Quote, "It should prove possible

00:16:38.710 --> 00:16:40.740
to apply procedures
adequate to that task here,

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:42.390
perhaps by permitting lawsuits against

00:16:42.390 --> 00:16:43.490
a subset of delegatetees,

00:16:43.490 --> 00:16:45.680
say those particularly likely to exercise

00:16:45.680 --> 00:16:48.420
the delegated powers or
perhaps by permitting lawsuits

00:16:48.420 --> 00:16:50.580
against officials whose
actions are necessary

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:53.080
to implement the statute's
enforcement powers."

00:16:53.080 --> 00:16:54.780
And that's also the gist of district court

00:16:54.780 --> 00:16:56.810
Judge Pittman's order on August 25th,

00:16:56.810 --> 00:16:59.680
quote, "While empowering
private enforcers SB8 limits

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:01.140
the defenses available to defendants

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:03.900
and subjects them to a
fee shifting regime skewed

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:05.070
in favor of claimants.

00:17:05.070 --> 00:17:07.490
For example, defendants
in SB8 enforcement actions

00:17:07.490 --> 00:17:09.670
are prohibited from raising
certain defenses enumerated

00:17:09.670 --> 00:17:11.880
under SB8, including they believed the law

00:17:11.880 --> 00:17:13.450
was unconstitutional that they relied on

00:17:13.450 --> 00:17:15.020
a court decision, later overruled,

00:17:15.020 --> 00:17:17.060
that was in place at the
time of the acts underlying

00:17:17.060 --> 00:17:19.880
the suit or that the patient
consented to the abortion.

00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:22.250
Section five of SB8 requires
state judges to weigh

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:24.560
the undue burden anew
in every case as part

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:25.960
of an affirmative defense inline

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:28.670
with SB8's new strictures
regarding construction

00:17:28.670 --> 00:17:29.990
and separability of claims."

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:32.480
Judge Pittman noted that SB8
doesn't leave state officials

00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:34.497
with nothing to do, instead the law as it

00:17:34.497 --> 00:17:37.060
has now codified, empower state agencies

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:39.190
to bring administrative and
civil enforcement actions

00:17:39.190 --> 00:17:41.480
against medical
professionals who participate

00:17:41.480 --> 00:17:43.360
in abortions that violate
the six week ban based

00:17:43.360 --> 00:17:44.870
on their state issued licenses.

00:17:44.870 --> 00:17:47.050
And under the state medical
practice act for examples,

00:17:47.050 --> 00:17:49.350
the state medical board
must initiate investigations

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:51.040
and disciplinary action against,

00:17:51.040 --> 00:17:53.600
as well as refuse to
issue or renew licenses

00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:56.770
to licensed physicians who
violate the provision of SB8.

00:17:56.770 --> 00:17:59.140
The state nursing and pharmacy
boards and health and human

00:17:59.140 --> 00:18:00.500
services divisions also perform

00:18:00.500 --> 00:18:02.160
similar administrative functions.

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:04.350
Judge Pittman's point was that
if providers were not able

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:05.650
to perform abortions,

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:07.280
they faced private enforcement actions,

00:18:07.280 --> 00:18:09.220
as well as professional
disciplinary sanctions,

00:18:09.220 --> 00:18:11.280
which are enforced by the state.

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:13.130
And although parenthetically
that when you involve

00:18:13.130 --> 00:18:15.100
the judicial system in enforcing things

00:18:15.100 --> 00:18:18.240
in other contexts that
clearly involves state action.

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:21.260
For example, the limits of
defamation law fall under

00:18:21.260 --> 00:18:22.330
the first amendment,

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:24.780
because it's the court that is enforcing

00:18:24.780 --> 00:18:27.330
defamation judgements and same with

00:18:27.330 --> 00:18:29.610
the courts inherent contempt authority.

00:18:29.610 --> 00:18:32.870
Often that runs right up against
the first amendment because

00:18:32.870 --> 00:18:36.130
it's the judge who is
doing something in court,

00:18:36.130 --> 00:18:37.660
that is a state action.

00:18:37.660 --> 00:18:38.493
In this case,

00:18:38.493 --> 00:18:40.310
both the fifth circuit and
the Supreme court bent over

00:18:40.310 --> 00:18:42.820
backwards to make sure that
this law would go into effect,

00:18:42.820 --> 00:18:45.720
which is probably going to
cause chaos within Texas,

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:49.080
because people just don't
know what's going to happen.

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:50.700
And what the status of the law is.

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:51.930
And we know that this is beyond the pale

00:18:51.930 --> 00:18:54.870
because chief Justice
Roberts wrote in his dissent

00:18:54.870 --> 00:18:56.270
that if nothing else,

00:18:56.270 --> 00:18:58.850
that the court should have
enjoined the law from going

00:18:58.850 --> 00:19:02.240
into effect so that
there was proper time for

00:19:02.240 --> 00:19:05.340
the case to wind its way
through the fifth circuit

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:08.410
and Supreme Court on
actual merits decisions.

00:19:08.410 --> 00:19:09.760
That was not completely
unusual for the Supreme Court

00:19:09.760 --> 00:19:11.540
to grant an emergency stay,

00:19:11.540 --> 00:19:14.690
which prevents the laws
enforcement until the case makes

00:19:14.690 --> 00:19:16.870
its way through the courts
and for a party to prevail,

00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:18.960
they have to show that there
would be irreparable harm

00:19:18.960 --> 00:19:21.690
and that they would be likely
to prevail on the merits.

00:19:21.690 --> 00:19:22.650
In a case like this,

00:19:22.650 --> 00:19:24.530
there would presumably
be irreparable harm,

00:19:24.530 --> 00:19:27.200
people who wanted abortions,
but were unable to get them.

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:29.720
In a party making that argument
would be extremely likely

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:31.770
to succeed on the merits
since a six week ban

00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:34.520
on abortion goes directly
against Roe and Casey.

00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:35.930
But the Supreme court's
majority claimed that

00:19:35.930 --> 00:19:38.160
these providers failed
to make a strong showing

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:40.080
that their legal arguments
would be likely to succeed on

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:42.510
the marriage, which is
basically telegraphing that

00:19:42.510 --> 00:19:44.710
the Supreme Court will,
when given the chance,

00:19:44.710 --> 00:19:47.430
overrule Roe vs Wade
right out in the open,

00:19:47.430 --> 00:19:49.100
when they have the opportunity.

00:19:49.100 --> 00:19:50.650
But here in this particular case,

00:19:50.650 --> 00:19:51.710
they're splitting the baby because

00:19:51.710 --> 00:19:53.020
the majority specifically states that

00:19:53.020 --> 00:19:55.600
the law raises real
constitutional questions,

00:19:55.600 --> 00:19:58.630
but punts because of the crazy
cockamamie procedure that

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:00.390
the Texas legislature created.

00:20:00.390 --> 00:20:02.470
This is completely nuts.

00:20:02.470 --> 00:20:04.410
One would think that if
you recognize the clear

00:20:04.410 --> 00:20:05.660
unconstitutionality based on

00:20:05.660 --> 00:20:07.810
the court's current jurisprudence,

00:20:07.810 --> 00:20:09.770
Casey is still technically good law.

00:20:09.770 --> 00:20:12.450
Then it would only doubly
mean that you should enjoin

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:15.147
this law if they have this crazy
Rube Goldberg machine built

00:20:15.147 --> 00:20:16.840
into this law,

00:20:16.840 --> 00:20:20.350
not letting them get a free
pass because of the machinations

00:20:20.350 --> 00:20:21.400
that they've created here.

00:20:21.400 --> 00:20:23.720
And in her dissent Justice Sonia Sotomayor

00:20:23.720 --> 00:20:25.550
did not mince words about this move.

00:20:25.550 --> 00:20:27.860
Quote, "The court has
rewarded the state's efforts

00:20:27.860 --> 00:20:29.410
to delay federal court review of

00:20:29.410 --> 00:20:31.310
a plainly unconstitutional statute,

00:20:31.310 --> 00:20:33.680
enacted in disregard of
the court's precedents

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:35.360
through procedural entanglements of

00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:36.620
the state's own creation.

00:20:36.620 --> 00:20:39.220
The court should not
be so content to ignore

00:20:39.220 --> 00:20:40.870
its constitutional obligations to protect,

00:20:40.870 --> 00:20:42.120
not only the rights of women,

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:44.050
but also the sanctity of its precedents

00:20:44.050 --> 00:20:45.480
and the rule of law."

00:20:45.480 --> 00:20:48.167
Chief Justice Roberts also
dissented the same lines, quote,

00:20:48.167 --> 00:20:50.990
"The statutory scheme before
the court is not only unusual,

00:20:50.990 --> 00:20:52.510
but unprecedented.

00:20:52.510 --> 00:20:54.750
The legislature has imposed
a prohibition on abortions

00:20:54.750 --> 00:20:56.280
after roughly six weeks and

00:20:56.280 --> 00:20:58.740
then essentially delegated
enforcement of that prohibition

00:20:58.740 --> 00:21:00.150
to the populace at large,

00:21:00.150 --> 00:21:01.710
the desired consequences appears to be,

00:21:01.710 --> 00:21:04.070
to insulate the state from
responsibility for implementing

00:21:04.070 --> 00:21:06.330
and enforcing the regulatory regime."

00:21:06.330 --> 00:21:08.340
Justice Briar disagreed
with the majority's view

00:21:08.340 --> 00:21:09.977
that his hands were
procedurally tied, quote,

00:21:09.977 --> 00:21:12.290
"Texas law delegates
to private individuals,

00:21:12.290 --> 00:21:14.770
the power to prevent a woman
from obtaining an abortion

00:21:14.770 --> 00:21:16.180
during the first stage of pregnancy.

00:21:16.180 --> 00:21:17.930
But a woman has a federal
constitutional right

00:21:17.930 --> 00:21:20.230
to obtain abortion
during that first stage."

00:21:20.230 --> 00:21:22.490
And Justice Kagan's dissent
called out her colleagues

00:21:22.490 --> 00:21:23.680
for abusing the shadow docket.

00:21:23.680 --> 00:21:25.900
Quote, "Yet the majority has
acted without any guidance

00:21:25.900 --> 00:21:26.733
from the court of appeals,

00:21:26.733 --> 00:21:29.080
which is right now
considering the same issues.

00:21:29.080 --> 00:21:31.600
It has reviewed only the most
cursory party submissions,

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:32.870
and then only hastily.

00:21:32.870 --> 00:21:35.330
And it barely bothers to
explain this conclusion that

00:21:35.330 --> 00:21:37.580
the challenge to an obviously
unconstitutional abortion

00:21:37.580 --> 00:21:38.520
regulation backed by

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:40.400
a wholly unprecedented enforcement scheme

00:21:40.400 --> 00:21:42.090
is unlikely to prevail.

00:21:42.090 --> 00:21:44.560
In all these ways, the
majority's decision is emblematic

00:21:44.560 --> 00:21:47.290
of too much of this court's
shadow docket decision-making,

00:21:47.290 --> 00:21:48.970
which every day becomes more unreasoned,

00:21:48.970 --> 00:21:51.050
inconsistent and impossible to defend."

00:21:51.050 --> 00:21:53.510
So regardless of where you
stand on the political issue

00:21:53.510 --> 00:21:54.390
of abortion,

00:21:54.390 --> 00:21:57.640
this is just bad lawmaking
and bad decision-making,

00:21:57.640 --> 00:21:59.430
it's bad for the rule of law.

00:21:59.430 --> 00:22:00.680
The way that the court went about this,

00:22:00.680 --> 00:22:02.570
allowing the fifth circuit to undo

00:22:02.570 --> 00:22:04.930
the preliminary injunction
that the district court put

00:22:04.930 --> 00:22:08.620
in place has created massive
uncertainty for no reason.

00:22:08.620 --> 00:22:11.260
The Supreme court will probably overrule

00:22:11.260 --> 00:22:13.590
Roe vs. Wade in the next term.

00:22:13.590 --> 00:22:16.740
Why go about this half-hearted
measure to do it on

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:19.300
the shadow docket ahead of time?

00:22:19.300 --> 00:22:22.410
It also leaves open all
kinds of new, crazy laws.

00:22:22.410 --> 00:22:24.880
It telegraphs to every
state in the country that

00:22:24.880 --> 00:22:27.480
they can get around constitutional issues

00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:30.510
and constitutional review by simply using

00:22:30.510 --> 00:22:33.760
the same private bounty
hunter enforcement mechanism.

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:35.090
And it could go both ways.

00:22:35.090 --> 00:22:37.240
Imagine if a state passed a law that said,

00:22:37.240 --> 00:22:40.410
if you aid or abet someone
exercising their freedom

00:22:40.410 --> 00:22:43.380
of religion, then you're
subject to a $10,000 fine,

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:45.360
and you have to pay the
other side's attorney's fees.

00:22:45.360 --> 00:22:47.000
Or on the other side of
the political spectrum,

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:49.920
if you aid or abet
someone buying a firearm

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:52.250
and exercising their
second amendment rights,

00:22:52.250 --> 00:22:55.010
then you are subject to
tens of thousands of dollars

00:22:55.010 --> 00:22:57.160
in fines, and you have to pay

00:22:57.160 --> 00:22:58.100
the other side's attorney's fees

00:22:58.100 --> 00:23:00.180
because only private individuals are going

00:23:00.180 --> 00:23:02.670
to enforce this anti handgun measure.

00:23:02.670 --> 00:23:04.180
But in reality, none of
that's going to happen

00:23:04.180 --> 00:23:06.050
because this was an ad hoc decision

00:23:06.050 --> 00:23:08.070
with one outcome in mind.

00:23:08.070 --> 00:23:10.620
If a similar law comes up
in a different context,

00:23:10.620 --> 00:23:12.540
the Supreme court will
just simply say that

00:23:12.540 --> 00:23:14.850
it's different for some ad hoc reason,

00:23:14.850 --> 00:23:18.670
and it will enjoin those
laws from going into effect.

00:23:18.670 --> 00:23:20.180
Again, this isn't necessarily about

00:23:20.180 --> 00:23:22.180
the political issue of abortion.

00:23:22.180 --> 00:23:25.620
It's about trying to uphold
consistency with the law

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:27.810
and the rule of law itself.

00:23:27.810 --> 00:23:31.000
This is terrible for all kinds
of jurisprudential reasons.

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:33.520
Though, as an aside, remember
that this is only with respect

00:23:33.520 --> 00:23:35.930
to whether the right to
an abortion or the right

00:23:35.930 --> 00:23:39.720
to choose is enshrined within
the constitution itself.

00:23:39.720 --> 00:23:42.600
There are always
legislative remedies here.

00:23:42.600 --> 00:23:45.190
Congress could pass a
law that grants exactly

00:23:45.190 --> 00:23:48.750
the same rights as
conferred by Roe vs. Wade

00:23:48.750 --> 00:23:50.730
or Planned Parenthood vs Casey.

00:23:50.730 --> 00:23:52.800
Of course a law can always be undone,

00:23:52.800 --> 00:23:54.870
but of course it's
harder to undo a law once

00:23:54.870 --> 00:23:55.710
it's been passed.

00:23:55.710 --> 00:23:57.930
And obviously there are
procedural issues with

00:23:57.930 --> 00:23:59.590
the filibuster that make it unlikely that

00:23:59.590 --> 00:24:01.550
the bare democratic majority in the House

00:24:01.550 --> 00:24:03.960
and the Senate would be
able to pass such a law,

00:24:03.960 --> 00:24:05.900
but that is always an option here.

00:24:05.900 --> 00:24:08.230
Now, the headline Roe vs. Wade is dead,

00:24:08.230 --> 00:24:09.810
might be a slight exaggeration

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:11.990
because technically
Planned Parenthood vs Casey

00:24:11.990 --> 00:24:14.270
is still the law of the land.

00:24:14.270 --> 00:24:17.010
But the law in Texas has been changed.

00:24:17.010 --> 00:24:19.970
And Roe vs. Wade, if not
dead, is on life support.

00:24:19.970 --> 00:24:21.680
And it looks like it's
only a matter of time until

00:24:21.680 --> 00:24:23.480
the current makeup of the
Supreme court finishes

00:24:23.480 --> 00:24:25.110
the job that they started on Wednesday.

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:26.600
Though, the Justice
department has now filed

00:24:26.600 --> 00:24:28.140
a lawsuit against the state of Texas,

00:24:28.140 --> 00:24:31.020
but it probably won't succeed
in any meaningful way.

00:24:31.020 --> 00:24:32.750
In the complaint, the DOJ
argues that the states

00:24:32.750 --> 00:24:34.610
have no right to quote impede burden or

00:24:34.610 --> 00:24:36.990
in any manner controlled the operations of

00:24:36.990 --> 00:24:39.310
the constitutional laws
enacted by Congress

00:24:39.310 --> 00:24:40.960
to carry into effect the powers vested

00:24:40.960 --> 00:24:42.370
in the national government.

00:24:42.370 --> 00:24:43.807
A state law is invalid, if it, quote,

00:24:43.807 --> 00:24:45.990
"Stands as an obstacle
to the accomplishment

00:24:45.990 --> 00:24:47.970
and execution of the full
purposes and objectives

00:24:47.970 --> 00:24:50.550
of Congress, or if it
directly regulates quote,

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:52.120
the activities of the federal government."

00:24:52.120 --> 00:24:55.030
The US has also sued to
assert other federal interests

00:24:55.030 --> 00:24:58.070
that they claim SB8
unconstitutionally impairs.

00:24:58.070 --> 00:25:00.820
The statute is alleged to
have unconstitutionally

00:25:00.820 --> 00:25:02.960
restricted the labor
department's job core program,

00:25:02.960 --> 00:25:05.080
the defense departments
Tri-Care health program,

00:25:05.080 --> 00:25:07.510
the office of refugee settlement,
the bureau of prisons,

00:25:07.510 --> 00:25:09.830
the centers of Medicare
and Medicaid services

00:25:09.830 --> 00:25:11.740
and the office of personnel management.

00:25:11.740 --> 00:25:13.440
And that's not surprising
because as we talked about,

00:25:13.440 --> 00:25:15.690
SB8 bans nearly all abortions in the state

00:25:15.690 --> 00:25:17.100
after six weeks of pregnancy,

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:18.440
which is in direct conflict

00:25:18.440 --> 00:25:19.777
with Planned Parenthood vs Casey.

00:25:19.777 --> 00:25:22.160
But the problem here is that
Planned Parenthood vs Casey

00:25:22.160 --> 00:25:24.080
probably won't be the law next term,

00:25:24.080 --> 00:25:25.430
when the Supreme court takes up one

00:25:25.430 --> 00:25:27.027
of several abortion related cases.

00:25:27.027 --> 00:25:29.050
And as we talked about
cases that are decided on

00:25:29.050 --> 00:25:32.090
the shadow docket related
to emergency petitions,

00:25:32.090 --> 00:25:34.140
don't carry any precedential value,

00:25:34.140 --> 00:25:36.790
but it does telegraph
what the various Justices

00:25:36.790 --> 00:25:39.600
are willing to do and in what context.

00:25:39.600 --> 00:25:41.710
And this is a pretty clear
signal that they're going

00:25:41.710 --> 00:25:44.500
to overrule Planned Parenthood
vs Casey and Roe vs. Wade

00:25:44.500 --> 00:25:47.900
when it comes up on the
official docket in next term.

00:25:47.900 --> 00:25:50.077
And regarding the bounty
hunter provisions,

00:25:50.077 --> 00:25:52.360
the DOJ asserts that
enforcement by private citizens

00:25:52.360 --> 00:25:54.280
makes them state actors.

00:25:54.280 --> 00:25:57.030
But again, here, the fifth
circuit has already ruled on

00:25:57.030 --> 00:25:59.700
that particular argument and
the Supreme Court dismissed

00:25:59.700 --> 00:26:01.280
the argument on the shadow docket.

00:26:01.280 --> 00:26:03.700
So it's extremely unlikely
that the DOJ is going to get

00:26:03.700 --> 00:26:05.680
a second bite at the apple
on that particular issue,

00:26:05.680 --> 00:26:07.777
especially when the fifth
circuit is already weighed in and

00:26:07.777 --> 00:26:10.950
the Supreme Court has
approved of that decision.

00:26:10.950 --> 00:26:12.600
And speaking of that court of appeals,

00:26:12.600 --> 00:26:14.570
the fifth circuit finally
wrote an opinion to explain

00:26:14.570 --> 00:26:16.730
this decision in whole women's health.

00:26:16.730 --> 00:26:19.670
This is actually unrelated
to the new DOJ lawsuit,

00:26:19.670 --> 00:26:20.850
but according to the fifth circuit,

00:26:20.850 --> 00:26:24.100
SB8 emphatically precludes
enforcement by any state local

00:26:24.100 --> 00:26:25.360
or agency officials,

00:26:25.360 --> 00:26:28.680
the defendant officials thus
lack any enforcement connection

00:26:28.680 --> 00:26:32.440
to SB8 and are not amenable to
suit under Exparte vs Young.

00:26:32.440 --> 00:26:34.240
Therefore the fifth circuit
of Pines that the plaintiffs

00:26:34.240 --> 00:26:36.925
don't have any claim against
the state licensing officials,

00:26:36.925 --> 00:26:40.240
namely the executive directors
of the Texas medical board,

00:26:40.240 --> 00:26:43.160
Texas nursing board, or the
Texas board of pharmacy,

00:26:43.160 --> 00:26:46.010
or the state health and
human services commissioner.

00:26:46.010 --> 00:26:47.980
The court says it's
mindful that SB8 violates

00:26:47.980 --> 00:26:49.080
people's constitutional rights,

00:26:49.080 --> 00:26:52.090
but that women can't sue
at this particular time

00:26:52.090 --> 00:26:54.040
and that they might be
able to sue in the future.

00:26:54.040 --> 00:26:54.947
Here's that language.

00:26:54.947 --> 00:26:58.120
"We are mindful that SB8 applies
to pre-viability abortions,

00:26:58.120 --> 00:26:59.970
which may raise serious
questions regarding

00:26:59.970 --> 00:27:02.000
the constitutionality of the Texas law,

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:03.980
the citation, but also see,

00:27:03.980 --> 00:27:06.297
noting the courts order
in no way limits other

00:27:06.297 --> 00:27:09.130
procedurally proper
challenges to the Texas law,

00:27:09.130 --> 00:27:11.340
including in Texas state courts."

00:27:11.340 --> 00:27:13.520
So effectively the fifth circuit
is saying that the only way

00:27:13.520 --> 00:27:16.420
to challenge this law is to
wait for a person to sue under

00:27:16.420 --> 00:27:17.610
the bounty hunter provision,

00:27:17.610 --> 00:27:19.610
and then raise the
constitutionality of SB8

00:27:19.610 --> 00:27:21.210
as a defense to liability.

00:27:21.210 --> 00:27:23.340
And the fifth circuit was
certainly not going to waste

00:27:23.340 --> 00:27:25.777
the decision of the Supreme
court, stating quote,

00:27:25.777 --> 00:27:27.870
"We are also mindful of
the real world effects

00:27:27.870 --> 00:27:30.830
while courts resolve these
vexing procedural questions.

00:27:30.830 --> 00:27:32.850
But we point out, as
did the Supreme court,

00:27:32.850 --> 00:27:35.570
the potential SB8 defendants
will be able to raise defenses

00:27:35.570 --> 00:27:37.220
before state courts that are bound

00:27:37.220 --> 00:27:38.710
to enforce the constitution."

00:27:38.710 --> 00:27:39.543
So unfortunately,

00:27:39.543 --> 00:27:40.960
there's going to be a ton
of confusion while we wait

00:27:40.960 --> 00:27:43.610
for someone to try to enforce
the bounty hunter provisions,

00:27:43.610 --> 00:27:46.540
at which time the defendant
will then try to claim

00:27:46.540 --> 00:27:49.440
that SB8 was unconstitutional
from the get-go.

00:27:49.440 --> 00:27:51.670
In which case, we'll be right
back in front of the courts,

00:27:51.670 --> 00:27:53.540
making all of these same arguments again.

00:27:53.540 --> 00:27:55.920
So do you need to vent
about the court's decisions

00:27:55.920 --> 00:27:57.540
or maybe call your Congress person,

00:27:57.540 --> 00:28:00.130
